WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Do you ever feel like a fake? Like you're not really qualified for the job you're doing despite your achievements and credentials? Or like you've just lucked your way into whatever professional or academic success you enjoy, but you're just one mistake away from being found out for the fraud you really are. If so, you're not alone. Those are signs of imposter phenomenon, also called imposter syndrome. While estimates vary, some research suggests that up to 70% or more of people experience imposter feelings at some point in their lives. So where do these feelings come from?
你是否曾觉得自己是个冒牌货？尽管你取得了各种成就和资历，但你总觉得自己并不真正胜任目前的工作？或者觉得你只是靠运气才取得了现在的职业或学术成就，只要犯一个错误，就会被戳穿你其实是个骗子？如果这样，你并不孤单。这些都是“冒牌者现象”（imposter phenomenon，又称“冒牌者综合征”）的征兆。虽然估计数据有所不同，但一些研究表明，多达70%甚至更多的人在人生的某个阶段都曾体验过这种冒牌心理。那么，这些感受究竟从何而来？

2
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Who is most likely to experience imposter phenomenon? What repercussions can it have in people's personal and professional lives? And if you're troubled by imposter feelings, what can you do to address them and keep them from holding you back? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills.
什么样的人最容易经历“冒牌者现象”？它会在人们的个人和职业生活中产生什么影响？如果你正为这种冒牌心理而困扰，你可以做些甚么来应对它们，并防止它们拖你的后腿？欢迎收听美国心理学会的旗舰播客《心理学畅谈》（Speaking of Psychology），该播客探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是主持人金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。

