terminology to the literature. We see in sort of popular discourse now the words, the imposter syndrome, which is more popular than the phrase imposter phenomenon. I tend to stay away from the imposter syndrome because of the word syndrome itself has such a clinical sort of orientation and innuendo, and it was not, again, it is not a syndrome in a classic sense of something that is diagnosable the way that we think about sort of diagnosis as psychologists. So I tend to use the word imposter phenomenon, but I recognize that in terms of popular discourse, the phrase imposter syndrome is much more known. Yeah, and I agree with Kevin. In essence, in the book, I sort of talk about the fact that, you know, it is initially coined as imposter phenomenon,
这一术语时所使用的语言。我们现在看到大众话语中使用的是“冒牌者综合征”这个词，它比“冒牌者现象”这个词更受欢迎。我倾向于避开“冒牌者综合征”，因为“综合征”这个词本身具有强烈的临床导向和暗示，它并不是——再说一次，它不是传统意义上可以像我们心理学家那样进行诊断的综合征。所以我倾向于使用“冒牌者现象”这个词，但我承认，就大众话语而言，“冒牌者综合征”这个短语更为人所知。是的，我同意凯文的看法。本质上，我在书中谈到了这样一个事实，你知道的，它最初被创造为“冒牌者现象”，

